{"id":"~Zhao-Heng_Yin1","name":"Zhao-Heng Yin","counts":{"accepted_papers":2,"first_position_papers":0,"co_first_author_papers":null,"corresponding_author_papers":null,"Poster only":2},"papers":[{"id":"DzAZfcPUiP","title":"How to Peel with a Knife: Aligning Fine-Grained Manipulation with Human Preference","abstract":"Many essential manipulation tasks -- such as food preparation, surgery, and craftsmanship -- remain difficult for autonomous robots. These tasks are characterized not only by contact-rich, force-sensitive dynamics, but also by their \"implicit\" success criteria: unlike pick-and-place, task quality in these domains is continuous and subjective (e.g. how well a potato is peeled), making quantitative evaluation and reward design difficult. We present a learning framework for such tasks, using peeling with a knife as a representative example. Our approach follows a two-stage pipeline: first, we learn a robust initial policy via force-aware data collection and imitation learning, enabling generalization across object variations; second, we refine the policy through preference-based finetuning using a learned reward model that combines quantitative task metrics with qualitative human feedback, aligning policy behavior with human notions of task quality. Using only 50--200 peeling trajectories, our system achieves 100% same-produce success on cucumbers, apples, and potatoes, with performance improving by up to 40% through preference-based finetuning. Remarkably, policies trained on one produce category also transfer zero-shot to selected held-out produce categories, achieving 50% success on zucchini, 90% on pear, and 80% on daikon radish.","summary":null,"type":"Poster only","topics":["强化学习与奖励","模仿与示范学习","数据与评测","触觉与接触","安全、泛化与鲁棒性","人机交互与协作"],"title_topics":["人机交互与协作"],"authors":["~Toru_Lin1","~Shuying_Deng1","~Zhao-Heng_Yin1","~Pieter_Abbeel2","~Jitendra_Malik2"],"institutions":["Tsinghua University","University of California, Berkeley"],"pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.03280","openreview":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=DzAZfcPUiP","project":["https://toruowo.github.io/peel"],"code":[],"poster_date":"2026-11-09","poster_time":"5:00–6:30 PM","poster_venue":"JW Marriott","poster_number":68,"hardware":[{"model":"Kinova Gen3","env":["real"],"reviewed":false},{"model":"Intel RealSense","env":["real"],"reviewed":false},{"model":"RealSense D405","env":["real"],"reviewed":false}],"has_social_sample":false,"role_evidence":null,"author_role":{"id":"DzAZfcPUiP","position":3,"first":false,"roles":[]}},{"id":"KdaClHHEUt","title":"T-Rex: Tactile-Reactive Dexterous Manipulation","abstract":"The ability to react dynamically to tactile signals has long been considered crucial to agile human-level dexterity. Yet contemporary learning-based VLAs for robotic manipulation generally either overlook the tactile modality or are limited to encoders with static cues, in part due to  the scarcity of diverse training data and standardized evaluation, architectural constraints in current Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models, and limitations of static tactile encoders. In this paper, we push the frontier of tactile-reactive manipulation addressing all of these limitations. We open-source a large-scale, 100-hour tactile-rich dataset collected via a novel, data-efficient recipe that prioritizes elementary motor primitives. To effectively exploit naturally high-frequency touch signals without sacrificing the existing capabilities of existing VLAs, we introduce a variable-rate Mix-of-Transformer (MoT) architecture equipped with a novel temporal tactile VQ-VAE encoder. We demonstrate the effectiveness of tactile-reactive policies on 12 manipulation tasks requiring delicate force control, deformable object manipulation, achieving over 30% higher average success rate than the strongest baseline.","summary":"围绕接触状态快速变化的灵巧操作，将触觉反馈用于反应式动作修正。研究多阶段训练如何帮助策略在手物接触中及时调整，而不只依赖视觉与预定动作。","type":"Poster only","topics":["视觉语言动作与基础模型","数据与评测","触觉与接触","灵巧操作与机器人手"],"title_topics":["触觉与接触","灵巧操作与机器人手"],"authors":["~Dantong_Niu1","~Zhuoyang_Liu1","~Zekai_Wang6","~Boning_Shao1","~Zhao-Heng_Yin1","~Anirudh_Pai1","~Yuvan_Sharma1","~Stefano_Saravalle1","~Ruijie_Zheng1","~Jing_Wang79","~Ryan_Punamiya1","~Mengda_Xu1","~Yuqi_Xie1","~Yunfan_Jiang1","~Letian_Fu1","~Konstantinos_Kallidromitis1","~Matteo_Gioia1","~Junyi_Zhang3","~Jiaxin_Ge1","~Haiwen_Feng1","~Fabio_Galasso1","~Wei_Zhan2","~David_M._Chan1","~Yutong_Bai1","~Roei_Herzig2","~Jiahui_Lei1","~Li_Fei-Fei1","~Ken_Goldberg1","~Jitendra_Malik2","~Pieter_Abbeel2","~Yuke_Zhu1","~Danfei_Xu1","~Linxi_Fan2","~Trevor_Darrell2"],"institutions":["ItalAI","NVIDIA","Panasonic","Sapienza University of Rome","Stanford University","University of California, Berkeley"],"pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.17055","openreview":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=KdaClHHEUt","project":["https://tactile-rex.github.io","https://tactile-reactive-dexterous.github.io"],"code":["https://github.com/ZhuoyangLiu2005/T-Rex"],"poster_date":"2026-11-11","poster_time":"10:30 AM–12:00 PM","poster_venue":"JW Marriott","poster_number":135,"hardware":[{"model":"Dexmate / Vega-1","env":["real"],"reviewed":true},{"model":"Sharpa 灵巧手 / Wave","env":["real"],"reviewed":true},{"model":"Manus 手套","env":["real"],"reviewed":false},{"model":"HTC VIVE Tracker","env":["real"],"reviewed":false},{"model":"ZED 系列（2 / X / Mini）","env":["real"],"reviewed":true}],"has_social_sample":false,"role_evidence":null,"author_role":{"id":"KdaClHHEUt","position":5,"first":false,"roles":[]}}],"topics":{"数据与评测":2,"触觉与接触":2,"强化学习与奖励":1,"模仿与示范学习":1,"安全、泛化与鲁棒性":1,"人机交互与协作":1,"视觉语言动作与基础模型":1,"灵巧操作与机器人手":1},"role_counts":{},"role_papers_reviewed":0,"profile":null,"portfolio":null,"needs_identifier_review":false,"affiliation_evidence":{"direct":[{"institution":"University of California, Berkeley","method":"author_superscript","author_marker":"1","institution_marker":"1","author_excerpt":"ai Wang1*, Boning Shao1, Zhao-Heng Yin1, Anirudh Pai1, Yuvan Sharma1, Ste",